# A two-column paper (#85, #86): line-end hyphens that join a word ("para-graph") and
# hyphens that belong to it ("task-specific", "state-of-the-art"), a link-dense
# citation line and a link split over two lines, a footnote, a figure whose labels are
# short runs of text, a sentence crossing from the left column to the right and one
# crossing the page break, a running header, a proceedings footer and page numbers.
# The text is original. Rebuild the PDF with
#   swift scripts/make-pdf-fixture.swift fixtures/pdf/paper.layout fixtures/pages/paper.pdf
page
title Listening to Documents Without Selecting Them
gap 4
center Ada Moreau        Ilan Park        Noor Haddad
center Unread Lab, Lyon
center ada@unread.example    ilan@unread.example
gap 18
column
heading Abstract
para
| We introduce a reading tool that speaks the para-
| graph under the pointer, with no selection and no
| copying. Unlike earlier readers ({Moreau and}
| {Kline, 2021}; {Park et al., 2019}; {Haddad, 2020}),
| it finds sentences in the page’s own text and fol-
| lows them with a highlight. It needs no task-
| specific setup, and it reads two-column papers in
| the order a person would.
heading 1 Introduction
para
| Most papers a researcher reads arrive as PDF files,
| and most of those are set in two columns. A voice
| that reads them aloud has to follow the columns,
| skip the running header and the page number, and
| join the words a typesetter broke at the end of a
| line. Screen readers already walk a PDF line by
| line, and they read it the way it was drawn.^1
para
| Reading the way a person does means undoing the
| layout. Lines become paragraphs again, a hyphen
| at a line end becomes part of its word or disap-
| pears, and the figures, whose labels are only short
| runs of text, are announced rather than read. Each
| of these steps is small, but together they decide
| whether the reading sounds like the paper or like
footnote ^1VoiceOver, for one, reads each line as it was drawn.
column
| its source file.
heading 2 Method
para
| The tool reads what the viewer exposes to assis-
| tive technology. A PDF viewer built on PDFium
| gives one group per paragraph and keeps the line
| ends, while one built on PDFKit gives each page’s
| text whole and measures only the runs between
| its links. Figure 1 shows the steps from the page
| to the voice.
figure 50 Page;Tree;Sentences;Voice
para flush
| Figure 1: From the page to the voice, one sentence
| at a time.
para
| Sentences are cut where a period ends them, not
| where a line ends. When a page ends in the middle
| of a sentence, the walk carries the sen-
footer 52 Proceedings of the Workshop on Reading Aloud, pages 17–18
footer 36 17
page
header Listening to Documents Without Selecting Them
column
| tence across the break and leaves the running
| header and the page number out.
heading 3 Results
para
| We read 12 papers in both viewers. In the best
| case, 96% of sentences came out whole and in or-
| der, and every figure was announced instead of
| read. Hyphens that belong to their word, such as
| the ones in a well-known and often cited state-of-
| the-art result, were kept.
heading 4 Conclusion
para
| A paper can be listened to without selecting a
| word of it. The layout is undone once, ahead of
| the voice, so the first sentence starts as quickly
| as it does on a web page.
column
heading References
dest refs
para flush
